---
import Page from "../components/Page.astro";
import { GUIDANCE_LABEL, REPO_URL, THEME_LABEL, fmtDate, releases, summary, type GuidanceClass } from "../lib/radar";

const m = summary.meta;
const v = m.validation;
const share = (a: number, b: number) => `${Math.round((a / b) * 100)}%`;
const urlOf = (id: string) => releases.find((r) => r.id === id)?.url;
const lowered = v?.by_model_class.lowered ?? { n: 0, agree_strict: 0, agree_lenient: 0 };
const perRelease = ((m.api_cost_usd / m.releases) * 100).toFixed(2);
const QUESTION_LABEL: Record<string, string> = {
	guidance: "Guidance",
	results_strength: "Results",
	outlook_tone: "Outlook",
	uncertainty: "Caution",
	demand_weakness: "Weakening demand",
	margin_pressure: "Margin pressure",
};
---

<Page
	title="Method"
	description="How the Earnings Radar reads results releases: the source, the exact questions asked to the model, how often it is right and what it cannot do."
>
	<Fragment slot="header">
		<h1 class="mt-3 font-serif text-3xl font-semibold text-stone-900">How it works, and how far to trust it</h1>
	</Fragment>

	<div class="method mt-8 max-w-2xl text-base leading-relaxed text-stone-700">
		<h2>The text</h2>
		<p>
			When a US company reports results it files a Form 8-K with the SEC under Item 2.02, with the press release
			attached. The radar takes that press release from EDGAR for {m.companies} companies of the S&P 100 (the list
			as of December 2020, fixed in advance), from {fmtDate(m.first_date)} onwards: {m.releases.toLocaleString("en-US")} releases so far.
			Tables and legal boilerplate are removed; the narrative is what the model reads. When a company files a
			second Item 2.02 within 20 days, only the first counts.
		</p>

		<h2>On demand</h2>
		<p>
			Any other company is read when someone asks for it. The service looks up the company's Item 2.02 filings
			on EDGAR, takes the newest one the model reads as a results release and the one before it, and asks the
			same questions. Each filing is read once and stored, so the same company is instant afterwards. It works
			for companies that report results in a Form 8-K, which leaves out most foreign companies. The service has
			a spending limit; when it is reached, companies already read keep working and new ones have to wait.
		</p>

		<h2>The questions</h2>
		<p>
			The reader is a decision model (<code>{m.model}</code>). Unlike a chat model it does not write: it takes a
			text and a fixed set of typed questions and returns a probability for each answer. Company names, tickers
			and dates are masked before it reads. These are the questions, word for word:
		</p>
		<dl>
			{
				Object.entries(m.questions).map(([k, q]) => (
					<>
						<dt>{QUESTION_LABEL[k] ?? k}</dt>
						<dd>{q.replaceAll("`press_release`", "the press release")}</dd>
					</>
				))
			}
		</dl>
		<p>The guidance question has seven possible answers:</p>
		<dl>
			{
				Object.entries(m.guidance_classes).map(([k, text]) => (
					<>
						<dt>{GUIDANCE_LABEL[k as GuidanceClass]}</dt>
						<dd>{text}</dd>
					</>
				))
			}
		</dl>
		{
			m.has_themes && (
				<>
					<p>And the theme questions:</p>
					<dl>
						{Object.entries(m.theme_questions).map(([k, q]) => (
							<>
								<dt>{THEME_LABEL[k] ?? k}</dt>
								<dd>{q.replaceAll("`press_release`", "the press release")}</dd>
							</>
						))}
					</dl>
				</>
			)
		}

		<h2>The numbers on the charts</h2>
		<p>
			A release counts under the guidance answer the model finds most likely. It counts as describing margin
			pressure, weakening demand or caution when the model's answer is at or past the midpoint of the scale.
			Quarters are quarters of publication: a release that comes out in February counts in the first quarter,
			whatever fiscal period it reports. A share over roughly 97 companies moves by one point with each company,
			so differences of two or three points mean little.
		</p>

		{
			v && (
				<>
					<h2>How often it is right</h2>
					<p>
						I checked the guidance answer on {v.labelled + v.unclear.length} releases, chosen so that every answer was
						covered. A second reader labelled them from the text without seeing what the model had said. That reader
						was Claude, another AI model, not a human analyst, so this is agreement between two readers and not a
						score against a ground truth.
					</p>
					<ul>
						<li>
							On {v.unclear.length} of them the release announces new ranges without saying whether they are higher or
							lower, so the text alone cannot settle it. They are left out of the counts below.
						</li>
						<li>
							On the other {v.labelled}, both readers gave the same answer on {v.agree_strict} ({share(v.agree_strict, v.labelled)}),
							or {v.agree_lenient} ({share(v.agree_lenient, v.labelled)}) counting cases with two defensible answers, such as a
							range narrowed around the same midpoint.
						</li>
						<li>
							On raised, lowered or neither, which is what the main chart uses, they agreed on {v.agree_direction}{" "}
							({share(v.agree_direction, v.labelled)}).
						</li>
					</ul>
					<div class="not-prose my-5 overflow-x-auto rounded-xl border border-stone-200 bg-white">
						<table class="w-full text-sm tabular-nums">
							<thead>
								<tr class="border-b border-stone-200 text-left text-xs text-stone-500">
									<th class="px-4 py-2.5 font-medium">The model said</th>
									<th class="px-3 py-2.5 text-right font-medium">Releases</th>
									<th class="px-4 py-2.5 text-right font-medium">Second reader agreed</th>
								</tr>
							</thead>
							<tbody>
								{Object.entries(v.by_model_class).map(([k, c]) => (
									<tr class="border-b border-stone-100 last:border-0">
										<td class="px-4 py-2">{GUIDANCE_LABEL[k as GuidanceClass]}</td>
										<td class="px-3 py-2 text-right">{c.n}</td>
										<td class="px-4 py-2 text-right">{c.agree_lenient}</td>
									</tr>
								))}
							</tbody>
						</table>
					</div>
					<p>
						The weak spot is "lowered": {lowered.n - lowered.agree_lenient} of the {lowered.n} releases the model
						marked as lowered were not. The disagreements, each with its filing:
					</p>
					<ul>
						{v.disagreements.map((d) => (
							<li>
								Model: {GUIDANCE_LABEL[d.model as GuidanceClass].toLowerCase()}. Reader:{" "}
								{GUIDANCE_LABEL[d.reader as GuidanceClass].toLowerCase()} ({d.note}).{" "}
								{urlOf(d.accessionNumber) && (
									<a href={urlOf(d.accessionNumber)} target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">
										Filing
									</a>
								)}
							</li>
						))}
					</ul>
					<p>
						The sample is small and it measures how often the model is right when it gives an answer, not how many
						releases of each kind it misses. The other questions have not been checked this way.
					</p>
				</>
			)
		}

		<h2>What it does not do</h2>
		<ul>
			<li>It does not predict prices or returns, and nothing here is investment advice.</li>
			<li>
				It reads the press release only. The numbers in the financial tables, the conference call and the slides
				are not in it, and many releases state a new range without the old one.
			</li>
			<li>Press releases are written by the company and are upbeat almost without exception. Tone is not fact.</li>
			<li>
				{m.companies} very large companies are not the market. Sectors with two or four of them are shown, but one
				release moves them a lot.
			</li>
		</ul>

		<h2>The experiment that did not work</h2>
		<p>
			This started as a different question: once the price has reacted to a results release, can the model tell
			whether the news is better or worse than that reaction implies, and so predict the following weeks? On
			1,529 releases from 2021 to 2024 it could not. Its judgement had no relation to the abnormal return of the
			next 20 sessions (weekly rank correlation −0.05, p = 0.11, with the wrong sign), and it was mostly the
			first-day move restated: it leaned "better" after a fall and "worse" after a rise (rank correlation −0.70
			with the reaction). Three rewordings of the question did no better. The releases from 2025 on, set aside
			for a confirmation test, were never used for one because there was nothing to confirm.
		</p>
		<p>
			What the model did do well was read. The same answers that failed as a trading signal traced the margin
			squeeze of 2022 and its fading, quarter by quarter. That is what this page publishes.
		</p>

		<h2>Cost and code</h2>
		<p>
			Reading the whole history cost about ${m.api_cost_usd.toFixed(2)} in API calls, and a new release costs
			about {perRelease} cents. The study's data is updated by hand, so the history shown for its companies
			runs to {fmtDate(m.last_date)}; the on-demand analysis always looks at EDGAR at the moment you ask.
			The pipeline, the questions, the validation sample and the dataset are in{" "}
			<a href={REPO_URL}>the repository</a>.
		</p>
	</div>
</Page>

<style>
	.method :global(h2) {
		margin-top: 2.5rem;
		margin-bottom: 0.75rem;
		font-family: "Source Serif 4", Georgia, serif;
		font-size: 1.25rem;
		font-weight: 600;
		color: #1c1917;
	}
	.method :global(p),
	.method :global(ul),
	.method :global(dl) {
		margin-top: 0.75rem;
	}
	.method :global(ul) {
		list-style: disc;
		padding-left: 1.25rem;
	}
	.method :global(li) {
		margin-top: 0.4rem;
	}
	.method :global(dt) {
		margin-top: 0.75rem;
		font-size: 0.875rem;
		font-weight: 600;
		color: #1c1917;
	}
	.method :global(dd) {
		font-size: 0.875rem;
		color: #57534e;
	}
	.method :global(a) {
		text-decoration: underline;
	}
	.method :global(a:hover) {
		color: #1c1917;
	}
	.method :global(code) {
		font-size: 0.85em;
		background: #f5f5f4;
		padding: 0.1em 0.35em;
		border-radius: 4px;
	}
</style>
